#: ../../source/guides/engine/build.rst:41
msgid ""
"On a fresh clone, ``make init`` is the whole first-time setup: it creates"
" the virtual environment, wires up the git hooks, and installs "
"everything. There is no bootstrap script to find and run first."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/build.rst:46
msgid "The extension and the binary are two products"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/build.rst:48
msgid ""
"One crate, two build products, and **neither implies the other**: ``pip "
"install`` and ``maturin develop`` build the *extension module*, cargo "
"builds the *binary*. This is the single most common way an installation "
"ends up half-done — the module imports fine and the ``freeports`` command"
" is nowhere — and it is why ``make install`` covers both:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/build.rst:59
msgid ""
"While working on the crate, ``make develop`` rebuilds the extension in "
"place. A stale ``.so`` is the usual explanation for a Rust change that "
"\"had no effect\" on the Python side."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/build.rst:63
msgid "Choosing where things go"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/build.rst:65
msgid ""
"Everything is addressed through ``ENV_PREFIX``, the Python environment "
"being worked on. If you already have one active — a virtualenv or a conda"
" environment — that is the one used; otherwise it is the repository's own"
" ``venv/freeports-dev``, which ``make venv`` creates. Neither case needs "
"a flag, and a third environment is one variable away:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/build.rst:74
msgid ""
"The binary follows the same rule: by default it lands in the active "
"environment's ``bin/``, so that ``make uninstall`` is complete and "
"nothing is left behind in your home directory. The cost is that it is not"
" on your ``PATH`` with the environment deactivated. For a system-wide "
"install, the GNU variables are honoured:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/build.rst:85
msgid "When something is wrong"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/build.rst:91
msgid ""
"It reports rather than repairs: which environment is in use and its "
"Python version, whether cargo is present, which of the three "
"distributions are installed, which commands are actually on disk, and "
"whether the external programs ``freeports-validate`` shells out to — "
"``gpg``, ``jq``, ``sha256sum``, ``realpath`` — exist. For each thing "
"missing it names the target that supplies it. It also recognises the "
"specific inconsistencies this repository has produced before: a module "
"installed without its binary, and packages left behind by an earlier "
"layout."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/build.rst:98
msgid ""
"``make installcheck`` is the shorter question — it runs the command and "
"imports the module, and says nothing if both work."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/build.rst:102
msgid "The commit gate"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/build.rst:104
msgid ""
"``.githooks/pre-commit`` runs ``make pre-commit`` — an alias of ``make "
"ci-fast`` — on a ``dev`` branch, and ``make ci-full`` on a ``prod`` one. "
"The gate is a *name*, not a list: what each consists of is decided in the"
" ``Makefile``, so either can grow without the hook being edited."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/build.rst:108
msgid ""
"**The fast gate costs 3.8 seconds** with ``make -j``, which is what the "
"hook passes, and that number is a budget rather than an outcome. It holds"
" lint, the two fast suites, the docstring walk, the report and the "
"verdict. Everything else — the instrumented coverage, the crate's "
"integration tests and doctests, the process-per-test half of the tooling "
"suite, and everything that touches the network — is run deliberately, and"
" what has not been run at this commit is *listed as not run* at every "
"commit. :ref:`fast-and-slow` is the reasoning, in one page: the value of "
"a check is how often it runs, so the gate is sorted by cost and the rest "
"is made visible rather than optional."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/build.rst:116
msgid ""
"**Whether a failure refuses the commit depends on the branch.** "
"``ci.yaml`` at the repository root puts every branch in one of three "
"classes, and only ``prod`` refuses; ``dev`` — the default — measures and "
"reports. ``prod`` also inverts the gate: it runs everything first, so "
"that nothing about a production commit can be stale. ``make branch-"
"class`` says which class you are on and the rule that put you there. "
":doc:`../devops/index` is the whole of it: every metric and every suite, "
"what each measures and what it does not, why an unmeasured figure refuses"
" on a production branch, and the fingerprint rule."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/build.rst:124
msgid ""
"Formatting is not part of it, deliberately. The git ``clean`` filter "
"configured in ``.gitconfig`` runs ``ruff format`` on Python sources as "
"they enter the index, so an unformatted working tree is a normal "
"condition rather than an error. ``make fmt`` formats both languages on "
"demand, and ``make fmt-check`` verifies without rewriting."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/build.rst:129
msgid ""
"Neither is the validation report, and it is no longer in the fast gate at"
" all. ``make validation`` runs the three of them — ``validation-report`` "
"to regenerate the badges, the block in ``README.md``, the seven pages "
"under ``docs/source/validation/report/`` and the coverage page; ``check-"
"grants`` to say whether the claims in ``validation/`` still hold; "
"``check-keys`` to say whether the granters' keys are published. All three"
" resolve pages over the network, which is nine seconds with it up and a "
"failure with it down, so they belong to ``make ci-full`` and to a "
"production commit rather than to every commit of a working day."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/build.rst:137
msgid ""
"None of them is a gate. Grant integrity **never refuses, on any branch** "
"— a granted reference output regenerated by ``make-tests`` is ordinary "
"work, and a contributor who does not hold the granting key could not "
"clear such a gate at all. What the grant check says is still worth "
"reading: an exit status of 3 means it could not check everything, and the"
" grants it could not compare are not vouched for by that run."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/build.rst:143
msgid ""
"What stands in for it at every commit costs nothing. ``grants.coverage`` "
"carries the commit it was measured at, so the CI report — which *is* "
"refreshed at every commit — paints it grey and names that commit the "
"moment it stops describing ``HEAD``. The report is allowed to be one run "
"out of date; what it is not allowed to be is out of date and silent about"
" it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/build.rst:149
msgid "Why not the Autotools"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/build.rst:151
msgid ""
"The question is a fair one — ``./configure && make && make install`` is "
"the sequence everyone recognises, and the Autotools are the portable "
"build system par excellence. They are not used here for a reason worth "
"writing down."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/build.rst:155
msgid ""
"What ``autoconf`` is *for* is discovering the platform's capabilities at "
"compile time: which functions exist, what the maths library is called, "
"which flags shared libraries want. Those are questions you ask while "
"compiling C. Nothing here compiles C. The crate's answers come from "
"``rustc`` and its ``cfg`` machinery and from cargo's dependency "
"resolution; the Python distributions' answers come from PEP 517 and wheel"
" tags. ``automake``, for its part, is built around turning lists of C "
"sources into programs and libraries with libtool — none of which applies "
"to ``cargo build``, which would appear only inside custom rules. The "
"ceremony would arrive without the leverage, and with it a dependency on "
"autoconf, automake and m4 for anyone touching the build."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/build.rst:164
msgid ""
"What the Autotools would genuinely add, we take without the machinery. "
"The **conventions** are adopted: the canonical target names (``all``, "
"``install``, ``uninstall``, ``check``, ``installcheck``, ``clean``, "
"``distclean``, ``dist``) mean what the GNU standard says they mean, and "
"``install`` honours ``PREFIX`` and ``DESTDIR`` so that a future packager "
"finds the handles it expects. The one genuinely configure-shaped job here"
" — checking for the external programs no ``pyproject.toml`` can declare —"
" is ``make doctor``, at a fraction of the cost and with better messages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/build.rst:173
msgid "Cleaning"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/engine/build.rst:182
msgid ""
"``clean`` deliberately does not call ``cargo clean``: deleting "
"``target/`` costs a full recompilation, which is not what should happen "
"when a target is run out of habit. It does know about the debris a "
"working session leaves around the tree — ``build/`` trees, caches, and "
"the ``freeports.log.jsonl`` a run at ``-vvv`` drops wherever it was "
"started — so that a run that writes where it should not is at least easy "
"to sweep up."
msgstr ""

